# Who has to approve what.
#
# Since release.yml started tagging on its own, merging a pull request that
# bumps a version number publishes an update to every installed copy of Crest.
# That is the intended behaviour, but it moves the release gate from "a human
# typed `git tag`" to "a human clicked Merge" — and the difference between an
# ordinary PR and a release is now three lines in three files that are easy to
# skim past.
#
# These entries make that difference explicit: any PR touching the files that
# decide a release needs the maintainer's review, whoever opened it.
#
# NOTE: this file does nothing on its own. It only blocks merges once
# "Require review from Code Owners" is ticked in
# Settings → Rules → the ruleset targeting `main` →
# Require a pull request before merging → Show additional settings.

# The number that decides what ships, and its two companions.
/product/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json   @LennyDany-03
/lib/site.ts                         @LennyDany-03
/CHANGELOG.md                        @LennyDany-03

# The automation itself — a PR that edits CI or the release pipeline can undo
# every guard above.
/.github/                            @LennyDany-03

# The design records. These carry the reasoning behind decisions that look wrong
# out of context, and a change here is usually a change of mind rather than a
# change of code.
/CLAUDE.md                           @LennyDany-03
/product/Architecture.md             @LennyDany-03
